]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The rapid-fire shooters were aided by a new gun, the Walther autoloader with a lightweight slide. It far outclassed the older American pistols. It was used by the German and Swedish teams and they dominated the competition. The competition was held in the same manner as in " [:a {:href "/results/51632"} "1932"] " – 18 shots in three strings of 6 shots, fired in 8 seconds, with 6-shot shoot-offs then at 6 seconds, 4 seconds, 3 seconds, and 2 seconds. Of the 53 competitors, 28 scored perfect 18s in the first round. Seventeen (17) shooters also shot cleanly in the 6-second round."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the 4-second round, five shooters hit all six targets to advance: " [:a {:href "/athletes/42598"} "Cornelius van Oyen"] " (GER), " [:a {:href "/athletes/42540"} "Heinz Hax"] " (GER), " [:a {:href "/athletes/44294"} "Torsten Ullman"] " (SWE), " [:a {:href "/athletes/42654"} "Angelos Papadimas"] " (GRE), and " [:a {:href "/athletes/44250"} "Helge Meuller"] " (SWE). In the 3-second round, van Oyen shot a clean round to win the gold medal. Hax hit five targets to repeat his silver medal from 1932, after having finished fifth in the " [:a {:href "/results/50027"} "modern pentathlon in 1928"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "8 second Round"] [:th {} "6 second Round"] [:th {} "4 second Round"] [:th {} "3 second Round"] [:th {} "2 second Round"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/42598"} "Cornelus van Oyen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "18"] [:td {} "6"] [:td {} "6"] [:td {} "6"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athle